/.codescout/peers.toml
# memories/, projects/*/memories/, and system-prompt.md are intentionally tracked
/scripts/node_modules/
/scripts/package-lock.json
.codescout/private-memories/
/temp-docs/
/.env
/.claude/*
!/.claude/skills/
# Hook-created, at any depth under docs/. `goal-stop-hook.mjs:16` does
# `mkdirSync(join(cwd, '.claude'))`, so a session running from a subdirectory drops a
# log dir there; the anchored rule two lines up cannot match below the root.
#
# SCOPED TO docs/ DELIBERATELY — `**/.claude/` was tried and is WRONG. It matches at
# every depth INCLUDING the root, and git will not descend into an excluded directory
# to reconsider a negation, so it silently kills `!/.claude/skills/` above. Verified
# 2026-09-07 in a scratch repo, four arms: current rules leak the log; `**/.claude/`
# appended ignores the skill file; `**/.claude/` placed first does too (reordering does
# not help, same descent reason); this scoped form is the only one that ignores the logs
# and keeps the skills negation. `git check-ignore -v` blames `**/.claude/` directly.
# The cost of getting it wrong is deferred and silent: the two tracked skill files stay
# tracked either way, but the NEXT skill added under .claude/skills/ would never appear
# in `git status`. Add a sibling scoped line if plugin logs ever appear outside docs/.
docs/**/.claude/
.fastembed_cache/
/.codescout/tmp/
/.buddy/*
!/.buddy/memory/

# Nested per-session hook state. The two patterns above are ROOT-ANCHORED, so a
# `.buddy/` or `.codescout/` that a hook creates in a SUBDIRECTORY was untracked and
# not ignored — the state docs/issues/2026-08-31-nested-hook-state-dirs-are-untracked-
# but-not-ignored.md describes. On 2026-09-01 a stray `git add -A` swept 22 such files
# into a commit, which is the failure that pattern invites: files nobody intends to
# track, sitting in `git status` as untracked noise, one careless add away from history.
#
# Scoped to docs/** deliberately rather than matching `.codescout/` at any depth: the
# repo TRACKS content under /.codescout/projects/ (see the note further down), and a
# blanket pattern would swallow it.
docs/**/.buddy/
docs/**/.codescout/
# Cross-process write lock file (per-project; recreated on activate)
.codescout/write.lock
# Its holder-record sidecar. A separate file deliberately — the lock is mandatory
# on Windows, so a contender cannot read bytes inside it. Needs its own line: the
# rule above is an exact match, not a prefix, so `write.lock.holder` slips past it
# and would otherwise be committed.
.codescout/write.lock.holder
/.codescout/call_edges.db
# Runtime / session files
.codescout/cc_session_id
.codescout/guide_hints/
.codescout/constitution-seen/
.codescout/tantivy/
.codescout/peer-audit.jsonl
.codescout/usage.db.bak-*

# Benchmark artifacts (regenerated locally)
/results-*.tsv
/.codescout/crash.log
/scripts/__pycache__/

# Personal Pi MCP config — holds API keys; see contrib/pi/mcp.json.example
/contrib/pi/mcp.json

# Scratch/working directories. NEVER commit: probe pipelines extract symbol
# vocabularies and path/prompt fragments from OTHER repositories (including
# client work), so the working tree carries identifiers that must not land in
# this repo's history. Durable findings belong in docs/trackers/, which are
# privacy-scrubbed by construction — with the single declared exception below.
/scratch/

# Raw evidence behind docs/trackers/bistriceanu/ — an external user's bug reports,
# delivered as a WhatsApp export. This is the one place under docs/trackers/ where
# the "privacy-scrubbed by construction" claim above does NOT hold, which is exactly
# why the payload is ignored rather than scrubbed in place. It carries three things
# that are not ours to publish in a PUBLIC repo:
#   - a two-year personal chat with a named third party (family, calls, private life);
#   - his proprietary "HMM" trading-system plan + a full Claude Code session transcript
#     from his machine (/Users/<him>/work/hmm), which quotes his source;
#   - a security assessment of a THIRD party's platform naming an unremediated
#     tenant-isolation weakness, its file paths, and a deploy poller that reports
#     stalled rollouts as green.
# The tracker beside it carries the codescout-relevant findings, scrubbed. If you
# need the evidence, read it locally; do not un-ignore this and do not quote machine
# paths, client names, or the third-party findings into any tracked file.
/docs/trackers/bistriceanu/raw/

# Retrieval-stack model weights (GGUF + HF source), bind-mounted into the
# dense/reranker containers via CODESCOUT_MODEL_DIR. Fetched or built locally —
# see docs/manual/src/concepts/retrieval-stack.md. ~870 MB, never committed.
/models/

# Local codescout search index (regenerated on activate).
#
# Deliberately NOT `/.codescout/projects/`: that tree holds 53 tracked,
# hand-authored per-project memory files (`*/memories/*.md`, `*.anchors.toml`)
# maintained by explicit `chore(memories):` commits. Ignoring it would not
# untrack those, but it would hide every future one from `git status` with no
# error. The index DB below is the thing that is actually regenerated.
#
# This comment used to end "and it shadows no cache, since that path has no
# untracked content at all". That was true in a fresh clone and in CI, and false
# on any machine where `memory(read|write|list|delete)` had been called with an
# unknown `project_id`: that created `projects/<id>/memories` for any string.
# Fixed at the source in c0bdeec7 — the id is now rejected — and that is the ONLY
# available fix, because a phantom project's `memories/` is structurally identical
# to a real one's, so no glob can hide the former while keeping the latter
# visible. Verified 2026-08-08 in a throwaway repo: under
# `/.codescout/projects/*/*` plus `!/.codescout/projects/*/memories/`, git reports
# a phantom's memory file exactly as it reports a real one's. Do not re-try that
# pattern here expecting it to suppress the litter.
# See docs/issues/2026-08-08-gitignore-projects-rule-premise-false-on-a-real-host.md
/.codescout/code-index.db
/.codescout/code-index.db-shm
/.codescout/code-index.db-wal
/target-release-new/
/.vscode/

# SDD isolated worktrees
.worktrees/

# SDD per-plan scratch workspaces — ledger, task briefs, implementer reports and
# review packages, one directory per plan. Scratch by design: the git history is
# the durable record, and `subagent-driven-development` deletes the directory
# once a plan's final review is clean.
.superpowers/

# Locally downloaded ONNX runtime shared library (windows-gnu / EDR workaround
# for the local-embed-dynamic feature). Fetched per-machine, never committed.
/.onnxruntime/
